"voice": "<k>voice_preset</k>: <v>'cornerman'</v>  (warmth <v>5</v> · humor <v>3</v> · directness <v>3</v> · energy <v>4</v>)\n<k>stance_school</k>: <v>polarized</v>\n<k>emphasis</k>: Data↔Sentiment <v>3</v> (balanced) · Process↔Outcome <v>1</v> (strongly Process)\n<k>corpus.school.stance</k>: \"Durability is built by lots of easy aerobic running; the long game beats any single sharp session.\"",
"llmcall": "<k>prompt_id</k>: <v>coach_message_v9</v> · <k>schema</k>: <v>2.0</v>\n<k>message</k>: \"The walk is a non-event. Recovery effort, RPE 3, effort score 32.2, HR averaged 104.5. With your medication hitting peak right around noon, that HR makes sense — not flagging it. The walk did what a walk is supposed to do. Done. Here's the story that actually matters. You've run **three times** this week. Three. Your form is sitting at +22.4 — fresh legs, fuel in the tank — and you've deployed that freshness to produce a training load **41% below your own baseline**. That is not a recovery week with intention. That is drift. That is detraining, and the data is already showing it. Across 52 comparable sessions — easy effort, flat terrain, warm conditions — your efficiency has slid **7.5%**. That is not noise. That is your aerobic base losing ground, slowly and quietly, every time a run gets replaced with a walk. You've logged ten non-run sessions this week and three runs. Walks are not nothing, but they do not build the engine. You know this. The fix is not complicated. Your fitness is 120.6. You have the base. What you do not have right now is the habit — the consistent, genuinely easy running that is the only thing that stops this slide. More easy runs, fewer substitutions. That's the process. Get it back.\"",
"contract": "<k>tail.headline</k>: \"Recovery walk clean — running habit slipped, detraining trend showing\"\n<k>tail.next_steps[0].action</k>: \"Restore consistent easy running this week\"\n<k>.next_steps</k>: <v>1 step(s)</v>",
