"voice": "<k>voice_preset</k>: <v>'cornerman'</v>  (warmth <v>5</v> · humor <v>3</v> · directness <v>3</v> · energy <v>4</v>)\n<k>stance_school</k>: <v>polarized</v>\n<k>emphasis</k>: Data↔Sentiment <v>3</v> (balanced) · Process↔Outcome <v>1</v> (strongly Process)\n<k>corpus.school.stance</k>: \"Durability is built by lots of easy aerobic running; the long game beats any single sharp session.\"",
"llmcall": "<k>prompt_id</k>: <v>coach_message_v8</v> · <k>schema</k>: <v>2.0</v>\n<k>message</k>: \"Alright. Five kilometres. This afternoon. The very same day that this morning's coaching message told you — with some conviction, and not for the first time — that the log was supposed to stay empty. The walk itself is, irritatingly, completely fine. Your HR drifted *down* across it (–9.6%, which is better than your own typical –3.9% for similar conditions), effort stayed easy throughout, and the risk dashboard is green without a flicker. If you'd arrived here in a vacuum with no prior context, I'd say nice stroll, see you tomorrow. But you didn't. Worth noting: you set off at 14:30, squarely in the window where your medication is known to elevate HR, so the higher reading at the start probably reflects timing more than exertion — it settled as you went, which is consistent with that. The walk itself isn't the concern. What is the concern is that this is now the third time the rest-day instruction has appeared in your log and been... not taken. Fourteen activities in seven days. Two of them today. The aerobic base is holding up — efficiency trend stable, drift better than your own norm — but that's not a permission slip; it's a credit card with a limit. Adaptation happens during rest, not *instead* of it, and a walk counts as a withdrawal. Tomorrow: empty log. No walk, no easy jog, nothing. The data will still be here when you get back.\"",
"contract": "<k>tail.headline</k>: \"Rest day ignored again — walk aerobically clean but the habit pattern remains\"\n<k>tail.next_steps[0].action</k>: \"Take a genuine rest day tomorrow\"\n<k>.next_steps</k>: <v>1 step(s)</v>",
