"narrative": "<v>DISABLED</v> — COACH_NARRATIVE_ENABLED defaults off (PR #403).\nThe section is empty; the Consolidation job never enqueues. (#402 recalibrates.)",
"voice": "<k>voice_preset</k>: <v>'cornerman'</v>  (warmth <v>5</v> · humor <v>3</v> · directness <v>3</v> · energy <v>4</v>)\n<k>stance_school</k>: <v>polarized</v>\n<k>emphasis</k>: Data↔Sentiment <v>3</v> (balanced) · Process↔Outcome <v>1</v> (strongly Process)\n<k>corpus.school.stance</k>: \"Durability is built by lots of easy aerobic running; the long game beats any single sharp session.\"",
"llmcall": "<k>prompt_id</k>: <v>coach_message_v8</v> · <k>schema</k>: <v>2.0</v>\n<k>message</k>: \"Here's the thing about being told to take a rest day twice on the same day: the implied commitment horizon is at least 24 hours. It is now two days later. I cannot prove you didn't rest yesterday — the data has a gap there — but you are here again with another run, which is *not not* suspicious. The run itself was fine, which I resent having to admit. Morning slot at 8:00, sensibly before the Lisdexamfetamine peaks, flat course, 5.1km in 31 minutes. You logged RPE 3 — easy, conversational. Your HR averaged 158 and maxed at 171, which reads as a moderate effort without calibrated zones — a notch above where your perceived effort sat. Balanced weighting is the right call here; a morning session before the stimulant hits its stride will push HR a touch, and RPE 3 is a credible read on how it actually felt. The number that actually matters: HR drift came in at 4.0%, against your own typical 4.3% for these conditions — calibrated against your own history, not a generic rule of thumb. Marginally better than your norm, and your norm isn't elevated, so this is a genuine sign the aerobic system held up cleanly. More broadly, across 14 comparable sessions, your efficiency has improved 25% and HR drift has dropped 42%. The base is quietly building well, and those are real numbers. Pace variability sat at 13%, which is on the loose side for a flat course, but seven brief stops totalling 20 seconds explain most of it — traffic lights, almost certainly. Not a concern for one run. The one pattern I'm watching: 14 sessions in the last 7 days. Your training load currently reads fresh — form positive, fatigue below fitness — so the body is coping. But stacking easy sessions without genuine space is the classic slow-burn risk for anyone building aerobic base. Easy mornings are good; easy mornings with no true rest day is how the positive trend eventually reverses. If you actually took yesterday off, good — do it again. If you didn't, the schedule wants a proper gap: not a short walk, the log left empty.\"",
"contract": "<k>tail.headline</k>: \"Aerobically solid morning run — drift below norm, trend positive, rest-day habit the watch item\"\n<k>tail.next_steps[0].action</k>: \"Take a genuine rest day\"\n<k>.next_steps</k>: <v>1 step(s)</v>",
