"voice": "<k>voice_preset</k>: <v>'cornerman'</v>  (warmth <v>5</v> · humor <v>3</v> · directness <v>3</v> · energy <v>4</v>)\n<k>stance_school</k>: <v>polarized</v>\n<k>emphasis</k>: Data↔Sentiment <v>3</v> (balanced) · Process↔Outcome <v>1</v> (strongly Process)\n<k>corpus.school.stance</k>: \"Durability is built by lots of easy aerobic running; the long game beats any single sharp session.\"",
"llmcall": "<k>prompt_id</k>: <v>coach_message_v7</v> · <k>schema</k>: <v>2.0</v>\n<k>message</k>: \"Right, so. The rest-day advice has now been offered, declined, offered again, declined more creatively with a walk, and today we appear to be in round three. I'm choosing to read this as dedication rather than selective hearing, but I am watching you. The run itself: you said RPE 3 — easy — and I believe you. The HR read moderate, which sounds concerning until you account for two things: it was 29°C at noon, and that's exactly the window where the stimulant you've mentioned tends to push HR up. Between the heat (confirmed as a high-confidence confounder by the data) and the medication timing, the HR here is not a useful fatigue signal. Your felt effort is the honest read, and it says easy. HR drift came in at 14.6%, well above your personal norm of around 2.8% for these conditions — but that gap is explained entirely by those two confounds. Discount it. The numbers that actually matter: your efficiency on comparable moderate runs in the heat has improved 27% over recent months, and HR drift in those same conditions has been trending down 56.5%. Both of those you've earned by consistently running, which is evidently not something you're capable of stopping. What the data hasn't absorbed yet is the recovery those runs require. Seventeen activities in the last seven days. That is not a training week; it is a controlled collapse in slow motion. No pain reported today, which is genuinely good, and the overall risk score is low — but the fatigue flag is up and the volume warrants it. The one thing that would move the needle here is a day with nothing in the log. Not a walk. Not an \"easy\" 3k. Nothing. That's the next step, it's the same next step it's been for three days, and I will continue saying it until one of us gets bored. The data says you can run. It also says you really should stop, just for today.\"",
"contract": "<k>tail.headline</k>: \"Rest day overdue — HR confounded by heat and medication timing; RPE 3 = easy\"\n<k>tail.next_steps[0].action</k>: \"Take a genuine rest day\"\n<k>.next_steps</k>: <v>1 step(s)</v>",
