"voice": "<k>voice_preset</k>: <v>'cornerman'</v>  (warmth <v>5</v> · humor <v>3</v> · directness <v>3</v> · energy <v>4</v>)\n<k>stance_school</k>: <v>polarized</v>\n<k>emphasis</k>: Data↔Sentiment <v>3</v> (balanced) · Process↔Outcome <v>1</v> (strongly Process)\n<k>corpus.school.stance</k>: \"Durability is built by lots of easy aerobic running; the long game beats any single sharp session.\"",
"llmcall": "<k>prompt_id</k>: <v>coach_message_v7</v> · <k>schema</k>: <v>2.0</v>\n<k>message</k>: \"Right, so \"take a genuine rest day\" was yesterday's advice, and what you've presented me with is a lunchtime walk. Bold move. To be fair: the walk itself was fine. 3.5km, easy effort throughout, green risk score, nothing the data can actually object to. Your average heart rate of 132 bpm is higher than you'd expect from a gentle stroll, but your walk started at 11:38 and rolled straight into the medication peak window — and you picked up 72 metres of elevation along the way, which isn't free. Those two things together are almost certainly what's inflating the HR reading, not any genuine exertion. There's no RPE logged to cross-check against, but nothing here is alarming. The problem isn't the walk. The problem is you've clocked 17 activities in the last seven days, yesterday's message said explicitly \"no easy session as a substitute,\" and a lunchtime walk is, categorically, an easy session as a substitute. Your training load currently reads fresh — fitness ahead of fatigue — and that's genuinely useful form. The way it stays useful is by letting the body do nothing for a day, not something light. Consistent easy-day discipline is the habit that compounds here; one actual empty day does more than another 3.5km of \"technically fine.\" Consider this the universe invoking the rules on a technicality. Rest day still owed.\"",
"contract": "<k>tail.headline</k>: \"Rest day advice not taken — walk was fine, rest still owed\"\n<k>tail.next_steps[0].action</k>: \"Take a genuine rest day\"\n<k>.next_steps</k>: <v>1 step(s)</v>",
