"voice": "<k>voice_preset</k>: <v>'cornerman'</v>  (warmth <v>5</v> · humor <v>3</v> · directness <v>3</v> · energy <v>4</v>)\n<k>stance_school</k>: <v>polarized</v>\n<k>emphasis</k>: Data↔Sentiment <v>3</v> (balanced) · Process↔Outcome <v>1</v> (strongly Process)\n<k>corpus.school.stance</k>: \"Durability is built by lots of easy aerobic running; the long game beats any single sharp session.\"",
"llmcall": "<k>prompt_id</k>: <v>coach_message_v6</v> · <k>schema</k>: <v>2.0</v>\n<k>message</k>: \"*Dear Reader,* Your correspondent must confess she arrived at this column expecting to write about nothing at all — and the evening ride has, blessedly, obliged. Thirty minutes indoors, an average heart rate of 141, an RPE of 3, and the quiet dignity of a session that asked very little and received exactly that. Easy discipline, delivered. You have now strung together three clean, low-effort sessions today, which this columnist notes with something approaching approval. The ride itself is beyond reproach. The scandal, as always, lies elsewhere. Sixteen activities in seven days. Three of them today. Each one impeccably easy, yes — but even easy sessions accumulate, and your correspondent has been keeping count. The training metrics are not yet shouting: fitness and fatigue sit nearly matched, form is marginally positive, and there is no aggressive ramp to report. But there is a quieter note in the data worth raising. Your easy, flat sessions have shown an efficiency decline of roughly 15% across the last four comparable rides. Not a headline. A footnote that has started clearing its throat. The standing advice — keep easy sessions genuinely easy — you have honoured admirably, and this column salutes you for it. The next step in that same process is acknowledging that *easy* is not the same as *rest,* and that with sixteen sessions behind you this week, a day of genuine absence from the activity log is not idleness. It is the adaptation you have been building toward. You have six days available per week; the seventh is not a formality. Let this be the last entry today. Tomorrow, consider making the log look remarkably, productively empty.\"",
"contract": "<k>tail.headline</k>: \"Third easy session today — discipline held, but volume and efficiency trend call for rest\"\n<k>tail.next_steps[0].action</k>: \"Take a genuine rest day\"\n<k>.next_steps</k>: <v>1 step(s)</v>",
