"beliefs": "<v>DISABLED</v> — COACH_BELIEFS_ENABLED defaults off (PR #403).\nEmits the empty new-runner form; no belief is read or written.\nStored rows kept intact for a recalibrated re-enable (#401).",
"narrative": "<v>DISABLED</v> — COACH_NARRATIVE_ENABLED defaults off (PR #403).\nThe section is empty; the Consolidation job never enqueues. (#402 recalibrates.)",
"voice": "<k>voice_preset</k>: <v>'cornerman'</v>  (warmth <v>5</v> · humor <v>3</v> · directness <v>3</v> · energy <v>4</v>)\n<k>stance_school</k>: <v>polarized</v>\n<k>emphasis</k>: Data↔Sentiment <v>5</v> (strongly Sentiment) · Process↔Outcome <v>4</v> (lean Outcome)\n<k>corpus.school.stance</k>: \"Durability is built by lots of easy aerobic running; the long game beats any single sharp session.\"",
"llmcall": "<k>prompt_id</k>: <v>coach_message_v6</v> · <k>schema</k>: <v>2.0</v>\n<k>message</k>: \"Yesterday the instruction was keep it genuinely easy. Today you went out at 5pm, ran 3.5km at an average of 155 bpm — 81% of your max — and filed it under \"Evening Run.\" That's moderate effort, not easy. The walk beforehand? Lovely. The run itself? You snuck right past the speed limit. Here's what's annoying: the data underneath it is actually pretty good. Your HR drift came in at -2%, while your personal norm for flat moderate runs like this sits around +3.5%. That's a 5.5 percentage point gap — your heart rate was *tracking downward* through the run, not climbing. Cardiovascular stability, alive and well. But the number I actually want you to sit with: your aerobic efficiency has improved 31.2% across 12 comparable sessions. That is not noise. That is your base building in real time — your engine is getting meaningfully better at turning effort into speed, and the consistent work is showing up cleanly in the numbers. Genuinely good. One mechanical note before you take a bow: cadence was 159, which is low. You want to be somewhere north of 170. Given your history with foot and shin issues, reducing ground contact time is about more than efficiency — it's one of the levers that keeps those old problems quiet. Nudge it upward gradually. Bigger picture: this run was sandwiched between a walk and a ride — 86 minutes of combined movement in one block — and you've got 16 sessions logged across the last 7 days. Form is in positive territory right now (+8.5, fitness ahead of fatigue), but that density only holds if the easy days actually rest you. No check-in on how this felt, either, so I'm reading the HR and taking it at face value. Next session out: conversational pace, start to finish, no sneaking. Your aerobic engine earned that efficiency trend by not going too hard too often — don't be the reason it stalls.\"",
"contract": "<k>tail.headline</k>: \"Moderate when easy was the brief — HR stability and efficiency trend genuinely excellent\"\n<k>tail.next_steps[0].action</k>: \"Run the next session at genuinely easy effort\"\n<k>.next_steps</k>: <v>2 step(s)</v>",
"validator": "<k>is_fallback</k>: <v>false</v>  →  passed all 8 rules\n<k>calibration.referral</k>: <v>null</v>",
