"voice": "<k>voice_preset</k>: <v>'cornerman'</v>  (warmth <v>5</v> · humor <v>3</v> · directness <v>3</v> · energy <v>4</v>)\n<k>stance_school</k>: <v>polarized</v>\n<k>emphasis</k>: Data↔Sentiment <v>3</v> (balanced) · Process↔Outcome <v>3</v> (balanced)\n<k>corpus.school.stance</k>: \"Durability is built by lots of easy aerobic running; the long game beats any single sharp session.\"",
"llmcall": "<k>prompt_id</k>: <v>coach_message_v5</v> · <k>schema</k>: <v>2.0</v>\n<k>message</k>: \"Look, it's a walk. RPE 1, zero pain, effort score 40.5 — there's only so much to dissect here, and most of it is \"yes, you walked at the correct speed, well done, gold star.\" But you're paying me to find the signal, so here we go. Your average HR of 121 bpm is high for a stroll — until you notice the timestamp: 12:35 PM, right in the middle of your Lisdexamfetamine peak window. That's almost certainly the medication, not any real cardiovascular stress. Your RPE 1 is the honest read, and it checks out completely. What's actually worth your attention is the week you've built around this walk: four hard sessions in seven days, another one just yesterday, 19 activities in the last seven days total. That's a heavy block, and the consecutive hard sessions are already tipping the risk meter — only just, but they're there. This walk is the right response. Keep it up. Your efficiency in easy comparable sessions has been quietly improving, up 7.7% across 33 sessions in this bucket, which means the aerobic base is genuinely building underneath all this traffic. That's the bit you don't want to quietly wreck by treating every non-walk as an invitation to push. The rest of this week needs to stay genuinely easy — RPE 2-3 at absolute most, conversational the whole way. You've done the hard work; let your body actually cash the cheque before you write another one. ---\"",
