"llmcall": "<k>prompt_id</k>: <v>coach_message_v2</v> · <k>schema</k>: <v>2.0</v>\n<k>message</k>: \"Zooming out from what I said in the opener: this walk happened against the backdrop of 5 hard sessions this week and 20 activities across 7 days — that's a genuinely heavy block of work. Keeping today conversational was exactly the right call, and the HR data confirms it rather than just suggesting it. Your -2.2% drift sits right at your personal norm for easy sessions in these conditions (your typical is around -3.8%), so the body was doing real recovery work, not just going through the motions. The 97 metres of elevation gain would naturally push your average HR a touch higher, and yet the session still tracked clearly in the easy band throughout. Clean. One thing worth checking: today's data shows the walk was part of a broader block that also included rowing (~18 minutes) and cycling (~30 minutes), putting total movement time closer to 98 minutes across all three. I don't have the intensity read on those two other pieces — did they stay genuinely conversational as well? If yes, the whole block holds up as solid recovery. If either of them pushed harder than that, the combined load on top of 5 hard sessions this week deserves more respect in how you approach the next couple of days. Looking forward: your efficiency on comparable easy sessions is up 6.4% across 31 sessions — that's real aerobic adaptation building steadily in the background, and it's earned. The way you protect that investment is by letting recovery actually do its job. With the load you've accumulated, I'd keep tomorrow, and probably the day after, firmly at RPE 2–4 — genuinely conversational effort, nothing that makes talking in full sentences difficult. Once you've banked that, you'll be well placed to step back on the gas.\"",
