"llmcall": "<k>prompt_id</k>: <v>coach_message_v2</v> · <k>schema</k>: <v>2.0</v>\n<k>message</k>: \"The RPE of 3 tells me you felt relaxed on the bike, and I'll take that at face value. But your heart rate averaged 150 across the full 42 minutes — against your 191 max, that's real, sustained moderate effort, not a gentle spin-down — and most of that time your cardiovascular system was working in a moderate to comfortably hard range. Feeling easy and working moderately aren't the same thing, and after everything that's already happened today, that gap is worth sitting with. Here's the full picture: five hard sessions this week, three efforts this afternoon alone, and a total training load over the past month that's more than doubled compared to the month before. That's a meaningful climb. The fact that tonight felt like an RPE 3 is actually a good sign in isolation — it suggests you're absorbing some of the work — but it doesn't change what the accumulated numbers say. I've already flagged \"go easy tomorrow\" twice today, and I know repeating the same instruction isn't compelling coaching. So let me frame it differently: the reason for a recovery session isn't that you're broken or exhausted — clearly you're not, tonight's RPE says exactly that — it's that the adaptation you've built this month only actually consolidates if you give it space to land. One genuinely easy session tomorrow (RPE 2–3, conversational, 20–40 minutes at most) doesn't undo the fitness. It's what makes the fitness stick.\"",
