"voice": "<k>voice_preset</k>: <v>'cornerman'</v>  (warmth <v>5</v> · humor <v>3</v> · directness <v>3</v> · energy <v>4</v>)\n<k>stance_school</k>: <v>polarized</v>\n<k>emphasis</k>: —\n<k>corpus.school.stance</k>: \"\"",
"llmcall": "<k>prompt_id</k>: <v>coach_message_v2</v> · <k>schema</k>: <v>2.0</v>\n<k>message</k>: \"You went straight from the tempo run into the row, which tells me this was almost certainly planned as a combined session — so let me read it that way rather than as a second act that wasn't supposed to happen. Together they add up to just under 40 minutes of work, and the row was genuine effort: average HR of 148, peaking at 166 against your 191 max. That's comfortably hard to hard territory for most of those 17 minutes, not a wind-down spin. Real work stacked on top of real work. One useful detail on the HR numbers: you've noted that your Lisdexamfetamine tends to push heart rate highest between noon and 3pm, and this row was at 5:47pm — well past that window. So the 148 average here is probably a cleaner signal than a midday read would give you, and it confirms the effort was solid rather than medication-inflated. What concerns me is the broader picture. Five hard or moderate sessions this week, 17 activities in seven days, and your 28-day training load sitting at roughly 2.6 times what it was the prior 28 days. That ramp is steep by any measure. The risk score on tonight's row is green, but that's the row judged on its own terms — the cumulative trend underneath is where the real flag sits. So the call stands, just shifted to tomorrow: easy or off. RPE 2–3, conversational effort, 20–30 minutes maximum if you move at all — or genuinely don't. I know easy days land about half the time for you, but this is the point in the week where absorbing the load matters more than adding to it. How are you feeling physically after the two sessions?\"",
"contract": "<k>tail.headline</k>: \"Moderate row completing combined session — 2.6× load ramp makes rest tomorrow essential\"\n<k>tail.next_steps[0].action</k>: \"Take tomorrow easy or off\"\n<k>.next_steps</k>: <v>1 step(s)</v>",
