"llmcall": "<k>prompt_id</k>: <v>coach_message_v3</v> · <k>schema</k>: <v>2.0</v>\n<k>message</k>: \"You did what was asked — take a genuinely easy day — and the body agreed. RPE 2, no pain, and your heart rate actually drifted downward through the session: that's exactly the recovery signal we were hoping for. Your HR drift came in at -1.1%, which sits comfortably within your personal range for walks like this (your typical drift in these conditions is around -3.7%). A touch less negative than usual, but nothing that registers as a concern — 101m of elevation across 5km will nudge things quietly upward even when the effort feels like nothing. Quiet hills are still hills. Worth keeping in mind: the walk started right at noon, which is your medication's peak window, so the absolute heart rate numbers are probably reading a little high. With an RPE of 2 and full alignment between feel and effort, the real read here is simple — it felt easy because it *was* easy. That's not as obvious as it sounds. Your efficiency on these easy comparable sessions has been building steadily — up about 8% across 44 sessions. That kind of gain is almost invisible week to week, and it comes from precisely this sort of day: nothing heroic, just low and consistent. Four hard sessions this week is a full block already. Tomorrow, keep it light again — a gentle walk, easy movement, or a full rest day, all of them sensible. There's no target left to chase this week; the work is done. Let the body collect on it.\"",
