"llmcall": "<k>prompt_id</k>: <v>coach_message_v2</v> · <k>schema</k>: <v>2.0</v>\n<k>message</k>: \"The rep pacing in this session was solid — a speed CV of 4.6% is tight, and the slight fade toward the end (your last rep ran about 7% slower than your first, at a ratio of 0.93) doesn't cross the threshold that would indicate a real breakdown. Work and rest came in nearly 1:1, and the overall structure held together across the reps. What catches my attention more is what was happening in the recovery windows. Your heart rate was dropping only about 4–5 bpm on average across the rest periods — and one of those windows saw HR go the wrong way entirely, climbing rather than falling. A system recovering properly should show more clearance than that between reps, and this pattern suggests your cardiovascular engine was carrying real residual fatigue into each subsequent rep. There's important context here: this session ran from 1:35 PM, right in the window when your Lisdexamfetamine is documented to push HR highest. The pipeline didn't flag a formal HR discount, but given what your profile says about the 12–3 PM window, the elevated readings — including those rest-period numbers — deserve cautious interpretation. Your perceived exertion on the day is probably the cleaner signal for assessing true intensity, which is part of why I'd like to hear from you about it. Layered on top of that: this was your fourth hard session in the past seven days, with hard work just yesterday. The 5.5% HR drift sits just above the general ~5% rule of thumb — without your personal norm established I can't say definitively whether that's significant for you specifically, but combined with the poor between-rep recovery it fits what a fatigued system looks like. The risk flag here is grounded. The wider load picture adds weight to that read. Over the most recent 28 days you've covered roughly 168km compared to 49km in the prior four-week block — more than three times the volume. Today's session is sitting on top of a lot of accumulated work. Tomorrow should be genuinely easy: conversational effort, RPE 2–3, the kind of pace where you could hold a full sentence without catching your breath. Not moderately easy — actually easy. I don't have any check-in data from you on this one. Given the session timing and everything going on with cumulative load, two things would really help me read this more clearly: how did the reps actually feel from your end — were you cruising at a comfortably hard effort, or were you working harder than you'd have liked to hold the pace? And how was your sleep going into this session?\"",
"contract": "<k>tail.headline</k>: \"Consistent rep pacing but poor between-rep recovery — load is high after four hard sessions this week\"\n<k>tail.next_steps[0].action</k>: \"Take a genuinely easy recovery day tomorrow\"\n<k>.next_steps</k>: <v>1 step(s)</v>",
