"voice": "<k>voice_preset</k>: <v>'cornerman'</v>  (warmth <v>5</v> · humor <v>3</v> · directness <v>3</v> · energy <v>4</v>)\n<k>stance_school</k>: <v>polarized</v>\n<k>emphasis</k>: —\n<k>corpus.school.stance</k>: \"\"",
"llmcall": "<k>prompt_id</k>: <v>coach_message_v2</v> · <k>schema</k>: <v>2.0</v>\n<k>message</k>: \"An RPE 4 on a run where your heart rate averaged 165 — that's a meaningful gap, and worth unpacking properly. Both signals are telling you something true. The medication window effect we flagged in the opener is real and almost certainly inflating the cardiac read, so 165 doesn't map directly onto physiological hard exertion the way it would on a cool morning without the medication peaking. At the same time, the HR data does show you were working at an elevated intensity for the full 21 minutes. My honest read: harder than a 4, but probably not as punishing as the raw heart rate implies on its own — somewhere in the comfortably hard range is my best reconciliation of those two signals. The standout in today's data is the HR drift: just 1.3% across the whole effort. On a hard 21-minute run, that's excellent — well inside the 5% rule-of-thumb. And it fits a real trend: across your comparable hard flat sessions, drift has come down roughly 90% over the last five data points. That's not noise, that's your aerobic system learning to hold pace without fading, and it's genuinely good to see. What I do want to address directly: this was your fourth hard session this week, coming essentially back-to-back with the recovery walks you'd just completed. The advice after those was to keep extending the easy work; instead you went straight into another hard effort. I'm not going to lecture — you clearly had the legs for it today, and the clean drift backs that up. But the bigger picture is important: over the last 28 days, both your activity count and total volume have jumped to roughly four times what you were doing in the 28 days before. The green risk flag and stable drift say you're absorbing it right now. That's exactly why the next two days need to protect that signal rather than push it further. Keep it genuinely easy from here — conversational walking or very light movement at RPE 2-3, around 20-30 minutes. Four hard sessions means there's a real adaptation in the queue, and it only becomes fitness during recovery. You've already done the hard part.\"",
"contract": "<k>tail.headline</k>: \"Hard effort, excellent drift — fourth hard session this week, recovery now needed\"\n<k>tail.next_steps[0].action</k>: \"Take the next two days as genuine recovery\"\n<k>.next_steps</k>: <v>1 step(s)</v>",
